# Tests and DI lifetimes

## Files

Test projects may place small fixtures, fakes, and documents in the same file as the test class. Production and sample code stay at one top-level type per file (`SA1402`).

## Assertions

Use NUnit and **Shouldly**. Do not add FluentAssertions.

## Arrange / Act / Assert

Every `[Test]` method uses explicit section comments: `// Arrange`, `// Act`, and `// Assert`. Put a blank line before each comment unless the comment is the first line of the method.

- One Act section. The Act is the behavior under test. Preconditions (builders, inserts, prior calls) stay in Arrange, even when a later assertion checks those values.
- Assertions (`Should*`, `Assert.*`) stay in Assert. Do not put logic or further system calls in Assert.
- Use a single `// Act & Assert` section when the action is the assertion itself, such as `Should.Throw`.
- Omit Arrange when there is nothing to set up. Do not add these comments to `[OneTimeSetUp]`, `[OneTimeTearDown]`, or helpers.
- Do not split a test that is one scenario. Split a test only when it covers two independent scenarios.

Shared containers, `AcceptanceServiceProviderFactory`, `ResultAssertions`, `RepoLocator`, and `PackagePacker` live in `test/TestSupport`. Do not copy `BaseIntegrationTests` into an integration project.

## Dependency injection

Any change to `AddMongoDb` registrations needs a unit test that locks the lifetime. Build the provider with `ValidateScopes` and `ValidateOnBuild`.

For each service, assert:

- **Singleton:** the same instance from the root provider and from two scopes.
- **Scoped:** the same instance twice inside one scope; different instances across scopes.
- **Transient:** a new instance on every resolve, including inside one scope.

A scoped or transient dependency captured by a singleton must fail `ValidateScopes` / `ValidateOnBuild`. Follow `ProvisioningDiRegistrationTests` and `AcceptanceServiceProviderFactory`.

Do not start MongoDB for lifetime tests. Use a placeholder connection string and resolve only the DI graph.
